// src/lsp/lsp_uri.zen
// A document URI, as a path the compiler can open.
//
// The protocol names a document by URI and `std.env.Fs` opens a PATH,
// and until a second file was involved the difference did not matter:
// `lsp_hover.zen` handed the client's URI straight to `Source` and to
// `told_at`, so the two agreed by construction and nothing had to
// convert anything. A build reads the disk, so this is where that stops.
//
// IT IS A FILE OF ITS OWN AND NOT A PAIR OF LINES IN `lsp_serve.zen`,
// for the reason `design_lsp.md` §6 gives about the same class of bug:
// a URI arriving as `vscode-remote://` rather than `file://` is a
// conversion that has to be right in ONE place, and a second copy of it
// beside the first is how a remote workspace silently hovers `null`.
//
// WHAT IT DOES NOT DO, said out loud because both are real:
//
//   - PERCENT-ESCAPES ARE NOT DECODED. A client sends `%20` for a space
//     and this hands `%20` to `Fs.read`, which does not find the file
//     and says so by path. Decoding needs an allocator and a hex
//     grammar; it belongs here when a path with a space turns up, and
//     the failure until then is a diagnostic and not a wrong answer.
//   - NO SCHEME BUT `file:` IS UNDERSTOOD. `design_lsp.md` §6 records
//     that a remote VS Code workspace sends `vscode-remote://<host>/…`,
//     whose authority is not part of the path. Such a URI comes back
//     unchanged, which fails at the read rather than by opening the
//     wrong file.

str, String = std.text
Alloc, AllocError = std.mem

// `file://` — seven bytes, and the two slashes are part of it. A
// well-formed `file:` URI has an EMPTY authority, so the path's own
// leading slash is the third: `file:///a/b.zen` is `/a/b.zen`.
FILE_SCHEME*: str = "file://"

// The path a URI names, or the URI itself when this file cannot say.
//
// A URI THAT IS ALREADY A PATH IS LEFT ALONE, which is what makes the
// two-file form of `zen lsp` testable: `tests/corpus/lsp/` drives the
// same server with paths relative to the work directory, and a
// converter that insisted on a scheme would make the corpus and the
// editor two different servers.
path_of* = (uri: str) str {
    scheme_at(uri).match({
        false => uri,
        true  => uri.slice(FILE_SCHEME.len, uri.len),
    })
}

scheme_at = (uri: str) bool {
    (uri.len < FILE_SCHEME.len).match({
        true  => false,
        false => uri.slice(0, FILE_SCHEME.len).eq(FILE_SCHEME),
    })
}

// THE WAY BACK, and `publishDiagnostics` is why there has to be one.
//
// Hover is asked about a document and answers about the same one, so
// until now every URI on this wire was one the client wrote and this
// file only ever had to read them. A build reports across the WHOLE
// program: a diagnostic names a module by its path relative to the
// compilation root, and the notification carrying it has to name a URI.
// So a path becomes a URI here, and it is the same seven bytes read the
// other way.
//
// AN EXACT INVERSE OF `path_of` FOR THE ONE SCHEME IT UNDERSTANDS, and
// that matters more than it looks: the URI built here is compared by
// BYTES against the one the client opened the document with, and a
// mismatch does not fail — it publishes a second, identical-looking
// file whose errors nothing ever clears. `file://` + `/a/b.zen` is
// `file:///a/b.zen` and `file://` + `src/b.zen` is `file://src/b.zen`,
// which are the two forms `path_of` produces.
//
// A URI OF ANOTHER SCHEME NEVER REACHES THIS. `path_of` hands such a
// URI back unchanged, `Fs.read` does not open it, so no module is built
// from it and no diagnostic names it. Said out loud because the guard
// that is not written here is the thing a reader will look for.
uri_at* = (a: Alloc, root: str, rel: str) Res<str, AllocError> {
    out ::= a.String("{}{}/{}", FILE_SCHEME, root, rel).try();
    Ok(out.view());
}
